 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Fixed-Pool Relocation](3094-fixed-pool-relocation.md)
-[3094-fixed-pool-relocation.md]: A strategy that redistributes a fixed budget of splats to areas of high reconstruction error without increasing the total component count. It identifies low-importance splats and moves them to local maxima of the positive reconstruction residual. Unlike Adaptive Densification, which increases the Gaussian count via cloning and splitting, Fixed-Pool Relocation maintains a constant budget by recycling existing components, making it suitable for memory-constrained environments.
+Fixed-Pool Relocation redistributes a fixed budget of splats to areas of high reconstruction error without increasing the total component count. It identifies low-importance splats and moves them to local maxima of the positive reconstruction residual, reinitializing them to explore new geometry. Unlike Adaptive Densification, which increases the Gaussian count via cloning and splitting, Fixed-Pool Relocation maintains a constant budget by recycling existing components, making it suitable for memory-constrained environments or scenes where the total number of primitives must remain strictly bounded [3094-fixed-pool-relocation.md].
 
 ### [Blind-Spot Cross-Validation](3102-blind-spot-cross-validation.md)
-[3102-blind-spot-cross-validation.md]: A model selection procedure that determines the optimal number of Gaussians (splat budget) for a 3D Gaussian Splatting reconstruction without requiring noise-free ground truth references. It identifies the point of diminishing returns where additional splats begin to memorize noise rather than signal by monitoring the divergence between training and held-out PSNR metrics.
+Blind-Spot Cross-Validation is a model selection procedure that determines the optimal number of Gaussians (splat budget) for a 3D Gaussian Splatting reconstruction without requiring noise-free ground truth references. It identifies the point of diminishing returns where additional splats begin to memorize noise rather than signal by monitoring the divergence between training and held-out PSNR metrics. Unlike standard cross-validation, it ensures the masked voxel's noise is unpredictable from its neighbors, guaranteeing that fitting the masked data lowers the held-out score if noise is being memorized.
 
 ### [Density-Aware Densification (Splitting Surfels)](4514-density-aware-densification-splitting-surfels.md)
-[4514-density-aware-densification-splitting-surfels.md]: A mechanism for surfel densification that uses a density-aware splitting criterion based on local inter-sample spacing and a screen-space gradient accumulation strategy based on T-matrix backward passes. It replaces the ad-hoc projected 3D position gradient used in 2DGS with screen-space gradients and replaces the global scale threshold with a criterion that adapts to spatially varying overlap conditions.
+Density-Aware Densification is a mechanism for surfel densification that uses a density-aware splitting criterion based on local inter-sample spacing and a screen-space gradient accumulation strategy based on T-matrix backward passes. It maintains the partition-of-unity assumption by adjusting surfel density as geometry evolves during optimization, similar to 3DGS densification. It replaces the ad-hoc projected 3D position gradient used in 2DGS with screen-space gradients from the T-matrix backward pass and replaces the global scale threshold used in 3DGS with a criterion that adapts to spatially varying overlap conditions.
 
 ### [Temporal Densification](9531-temporal-densification.md)
-[9531-temporal-densification.md]: A strategy that detects and resolves reconstruction errors caused by object or camera motion by splitting Gaussians based on the variance of their normalized image error across time segments. It addresses the limitation of static spatial densification by identifying Gaussians whose error fluctuates significantly between keyframes.
+Temporal Densification is a strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that detects and resolves reconstruction errors caused by object or camera motion by splitting Gaussians based on the variance of their normalized image error across time segments. It addresses the limitation of static spatial densification, which assumes Gaussians are stationary, by identifying Gaussians whose error fluctuates significantly between keyframes, indicating they fail to track motion.
 
 ### Density-preserving Splitting
-A variant that discards cloning operations and relies only on splitting operations to avoid loss function perturbations caused by cloning [S6]. It modifies the splitting operation to be performed along the longest principal axis, shifting means by $\pm \alpha \sigma$ (where $\alpha=0.3$) and adjusting sizes and opacity to ensure the resulting density of the sum of the two children is close to the density of the mother Gaussian [S6].
+Density-preserving Splitting is a densification strategy that discards cloning operations and relies only on splitting operations to avoid loss function perturbations [S6]. Inspired by the observation that original splitting operations cause perturbations in the loss function, this variant modifies the splitting operation so that the resulting density after splitting is similar to the density of the Gaussian being split. Splitting is performed along the longest principal axis, by shifting means by $\pm \alpha \sigma$, where $\alpha$ is a constant (set to 0.3) and $\sigma$ is the size along the splitting axis. To ensure that the resulting density of the sum of the two children is close to the density of the mother Gaussian, the sizes and opacity of the split Gaussians are adjusted [S6].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Densification is executed every $K$ iterations (typically 100). The sorting step for selecting top candidates is $O(N \log N)$, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Cloning and splitting are $O(N_{ops})$ where $N_{ops}$ is the number of modified primitives. Overall, it adds a small overhead per step relative to the rendering and gradient computation cost.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The number of Gaussians $N$ grows over time, bounded by $N_{max}$. Memory usage scales linearly with $N$.
-*   **Performance:** The method effectively captures fine details (via cloning) and resolves blurry regions (via splitting) [S2, unofficial]. Ablation studies show that disabling splitting leads to blurry backgrounds, while disabling cloning leads to loss of thin structures like bicycle spokes [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; adapts density to scene complexity; no need for manual tuning of density grids.
-*   **Limitations:** Can lead to unbounded growth if $N_{max}$ is not set; gradient cancellation issues can occur if raw vectors are accumulated instead of magnitudes [S2, unofficial]; cloning can introduce artifacts if not handled carefully (e.g., intensity halving) [S6].
-*   **When to use:** Standard 3D Gaussian Splatting training where scene complexity varies and a fixed initialization is insufficient.
-*   **When not to use:** Memory-constrained environments requiring a fixed primitive count (use [Fixed-Pool Relocation](3094-fixed-pool-relocation.md)) or scenes with significant motion (use [Temporal Densification](9531-temporal-densification.md)).
+*   **Time Complexity:** Densification is performed periodically (e.g., every 100 iterations), so the overhead is amortized. The sorting and masking operations are $O(N)$ per step [S4].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The number of Gaussians $N$ is bounded by $n_{max}$ (default 500,000) [S4].
+*   **Performance:** Adaptive densification significantly improves reconstruction quality by capturing fine details and thin structures that a fixed initialization cannot represent [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Automatically adapts to scene complexity; prevents over-smoothing in detailed regions; removes floating artifacts via pruning [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of thresholds ($\tau, \theta$); prone to gradient collision issues if not handled (e.g., using Absgrad) [S5, unofficial]; can lead to unbounded growth if $n_{max}$ is not enforced [S4].
+*   **When to use:** Standard 3D Gaussian Splatting training where high-fidelity reconstruction is required and memory allows for dynamic growth.
+*   **When not to use:** Memory-constrained environments (consider Fixed-Pool Relocation) or scenes with significant motion (consider Temporal Densification) [3094-fixed-pool-relocation.md, 9531-temporal-densification.md].
 
 ## Background
-Adaptive Densification was introduced as part of the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1][S5, unofficial]. It was designed to overcome the limitations of fixed-point-cloud representations by allowing the Gaussian representation to grow and shrink dynamically during optimization. The method draws inspiration from adaptive mesh refinement and previous work on probabilistic densification using MCMC [S3]. It has become the standard densification strategy for 3DGS and is the basis for many subsequent variants [S5, unofficial].
+Adaptive Densification was introduced by Kerbl et al. in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper (2023) [S5, unofficial]. It was designed to address the sparse initialization problem, where a fixed set of Gaussians cannot adequately represent complex scenes. The method builds on the idea that gradient descent alone is insufficient for creating new primitives, necessitating a discrete densification step [S2, unofficial]. It has since become a standard component of 3D Gaussian Splatting pipelines and has inspired numerous variants and improvements [S1].
 
